---
/**
 * Regex Lab tool page.
 */
import ToolLayout from '../../layouts/ToolLayout.astro';
import RegexLab from '../../components/tools/RegexLab';
---

<ToolLayout slug="regex-lab">
  <RegexLab client:load />

  <Fragment slot="about">
    <h2>The problem</h2>
    <p>
      A regex tester answers &ldquo;does this pattern match&rdquo;. That is usually not the
      question. The question is more often &ldquo;show me the lines with X but not Y&rdquo;, which
      is expressible as one regex only through a negative lookahead, and the result is write-only:
      nobody reads <code>^(?!.*health).*GET.*$</code> back a week later and knows what it was for.
    </p>
    <p>
      There is a second problem that most testers ignore. JavaScript&rsquo;s regex engine
      backtracks, so a pattern like <code>(a+)+b</code> against a long run of <code>a</code> takes exponential
      time, and there is no timeout to set. A locked-up tab is the worst failure this kind of page can
      have, because the pasted input goes with it.
    </p>

    <h2>The approach</h2>
    <p>
      Four modes over one input. Match highlights hits in place with a capture table. Replace
      previews the substitution <em>and counts it</em>, which the native
      <code>String.replace</code> will not tell you, so the count is done through the callback form. Filter
      is <code>grep</code> with two lists, one that every line must match and one that no line may. Explain
      annotates the pattern piece by piece, which is what you want when you have inherited one.
    </p>
    <p>
      The hang is handled honestly. Three defences, in order of how much they buy: a match count and
      an input cap, which bound the linear cost; a deadline checked between matches, which catches a
      pattern that is slow across many matches; and a static warning on nested unbounded
      quantifiers, which is the only one that helps in the case that actually matters. The first two
      cannot save a tab from a single catastrophic match, because once the engine is inside one,
      nothing in the page runs. So a pattern shaped that way is held, the reason is stated, and
      running it is a deliberate click.
    </p>
    <p>
      Two smaller things that go wrong in every hand-written match loop are handled here. A
      zero-length match leaves <code>lastIndex</code> where it was, so a pattern as innocent as
      <code>\b</code> loops forever without an explicit step forward, and the step is by code point so
      it cannot split a surrogate pair. And the <code>d</code> flag is always set, which is what makes
      per-group highlighting exact rather than approximate.
    </p>

    <h2>Where it stops being trustworthy</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>This is JavaScript&rsquo;s engine, not Python&rsquo;s or PCRE.</strong> Atomic groups,
        possessive quantifiers, recursion and <code>\K</code> do not exist here. A pattern that works
        here works in a browser and in Node, and may need translating for
        <code>re</code> or <code>grep -P</code>.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>The risk check is a heuristic, kept deliberately narrow.</strong> It looks for a quantified
        group whose body ends in a quantifier, and for a repeated alternation with overlapping branches.
        A pattern that hangs some other way will not be flagged, and a warning that fired on ordinary
        regexes would be ignored, which protects nobody.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Input and match counts are capped</strong> at 400,000 characters and 5,000 matches, with
        a 1.5 second budget. Past any of them the result is truncated and labelled, and the statistics
        describe what was collected rather than the whole input.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>The starting patterns are pragmatic, not canonical.</strong> Each says where it is wrong.
        The email pattern rejects quoted local parts, which are legal. The timestamp pattern matches 2026-02-31,
        because calendar validity is not something a regex should be asked to know.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Explain is a scanner, not a parser.</strong> It labels each piece and cannot report nesting
        depth or tell you which group number a bracket opened.
      </li>
    </ul>
  </Fragment>
</ToolLayout>
